{id:"BG001",kind:"mathematical",at:"/inference/argument/0/text",fact:"If a complex vector space V has finite dimension n and a family indexed by J is a complex basis of V, then J is finite of cardinal n. If the same family is an integer basis of M, any bijection Fin(n)→J reindexes both bases simultaneously.",grounds:[{location:"/inputs/3/statement",reason:"H004 gives finite complex dimension λ+1."},{location:"/inputs/4/statement",reason:"H006 gives a single integer basis whose images are a complex basis."},{location:"/inputs/2/statement",reason:"H003 gives λ≥0 and hence ell=λ as integers."},{location:"/definitions/1",reason:"n=ell+1."},{location:"/definitions/2",reason:"I=Fin(n)."}],justification:"The finite exchange argument bounds every independent family by the size of a finite basis. For completeness, expand a fixed n-element basis in the given basis; the union of these finite supports spans V and independence forces every given basis vector into that union. Both bases are now finite, and exchange in both directions makes their cardinalities equal to n. Transport along a bijection of their index sets preserves expansion uniqueness over both scalar rings."},
{id:"BG002",kind:"mathematical",at:"/inference/argument/1/text",fact:"Every field satisfies the strong rank condition. A basis indexed by a finite nonempty set spans a finite-dimensional nonzero vector space, and no member of that basis is zero.",grounds:[{location:"/parameters/1",reason:"K is a field."},{location:"/inference/argument/0/produces/0",reason:"F001 gives n>0 and the I-indexed basis b."},{location:"/definitions/2",reason:"I=Fin(n) is finite and contains 0 when n>0."},{location:"/inputs/6/statement",reason:"M003 supplies the scalar-extended K-basis β and its values."}],justification:"For a surjection K^a→K^b, the images of the a coordinate vectors span K^b; finite exchange gives b≤a, the strong rank condition required by M006. A finite basis is a finite spanning family, hence gives finite-dimensionality. If β_0 were zero, the finite relation with coefficient 1 at 0 and 0 elsewhere would violate independence since 1≠0 in a field."},
